;; Standalone microVM runner for coding agents — no relation to Docker
;; Desktop, and it leaves the OrbStack docker socket alone.
{:pkg/brew {"docker/tap/sbx" {:trust true :cask true}}
 ;; Kits are not installed anywhere. ct and the build scripts both pass --kit
 ;; with a path into this directory, so a kit needs no well-known location.
 ;;
 ;; Commit signing comes from the external git-ssh-sign kit, whose source has to
 ;; be allowlisted once on the host — sbx settings, not a file syn can own:
 ;;   sbx settings set kit.allowedSources '["docker.io/","github.com/docker/"]'
 ;;
 ;; One kit and one template, so every sandbox is the same whatever it is working
 ;; on. What a single repository needs goes into osm-base with everything else.
 ;;
 ;; A template is a cache of running that kit, nothing more.
 ;;
 ;; git-ssh-sign is deliberately absent: it resolves the signing key from the
 ;; forwarded agent at signing time, so it has nothing to snapshot. ct applies it
 ;; at create either way.
 ;;
 ;; :sbx-kit-claude-config is required so a build applies the kit ct will apply,
 ;; not a half-populated one. It is not required because files/ ends up in the
 ;; template — it does not; a kit's files/ tree is copied from the kit at every
 ;; create, which is also why the freshness check ignores it.
 :pkg/script
 ;; The kit is named, not pathed: both scripts resolve it under cfg/sbx/kits from
 ;; their own location, so nothing here depends on the symlinks or on syn's `cd`.
 {:sbx-template-osm-base
  {:src "scripts/sbx-build-template.sh osm-base osm-base"
   :check "scripts/sbx-template-fresh.sh osm-base osm-base"
   :dep/requires #{[:pkg/brew "docker/tap/sbx"]
                   [:pkg/script :sbx-kit-claude-config]}}}}
